  "preamble": "Languages lacking /f/, or speakers who do not use it, replace it with the aspirated bilabial stop /pʰ/, which keeps voicelessness, labiality and a noisy release. Rural and older Hindi speakers pronounce Perso-Arabic and English /f/ as /pʰ/ (फ़सल /fəsəl/ ~ /pʰəsəl/ 'crop'), although in Hindi the dominant modern trend runs the other way, with /f/ spreading at the expense of /pʰ/.",
